lesson='## Deepwell brine, planet and depth candidate — 2026-10-25\n\nThe user calls v1 "a decent first pass" but says it "needs a good bit more" and asks to revisit every part. Do not interpret "300% better" as a measurable improvement or declare acceptance from the request. V2 revisits the water direction distribution and reflection detail, two-depth sky transport, foreground cloud veiling of the fixed planet, separate basin-depth airflow and mineral-surface drift, functional instruments and practical/daylight coupling. The v1 artwork remains fixed; the big quiet halo around its planet is replaced with a close guard plus source-derived front clouds. The original planet-free sky remains the sampling source to prevent duplicates. The first v2 soft density fields passed technical checks but were too faint in assistant still review; explicit terrain-guided wisps with moving centres, internal curl, widening and zero-slope fade resets replaced them. A long effect list or more masks is insufficient evidence of readable motion.\n\nEarlier v1 findings still apply: a water colour gate throughout the channel froze warm reflected buildings; restrict material gating to mapped shore boundaries. Rounded UI positions/bars caused a seam spike; continuous needle fields and fractional coverage fixed it without loosening the gate. Finer filtered waves retained more brine detail than the initial broad, soft study. V2 retains complete apertures and coupled emitter/spill, preserves steady windows and fixed machinery, and confines daylight change to real table/ledge highlights. Full-frame exposure pumping and invented hardware are not improvements. All versions and exact feedback remain preserved. Current v2 technical checks include complete decodes, local/quiet-neighbour seams, retained-layer controls, direct colour-aware YUV comparisons and exact v1 preservation. Assistant inspected temporal/decoded stills, not continuous playback; user artistic verdict remains pending. [Current v2 evidence](../music/deepwell-animation-v2/README.md), [v1 history](../music/deepwell-animation-v1/README.md).\n'
lesson+='\nApply foreground-structure occlusion to ground drift as well as airborne haze: the final v2 check caught 34 salt-drift pixels on a pipeline support even though the air layers already excluded it. Share the support blocker across the relevant weather masks and assert named pipeline/support protection on every rendered source frame. The affected atmosphere/composite were rerendered; checks were not loosened. Exact failed candidate and diagnosis remain in v2 history.\n'
new=old[:start]+lesson+old[end:];updates[path]=(old,new)
